{# The self-download control, shared by documents and decks.

   WHAT DOWNLOADS. A generated page is already fully self-contained -- assets
   inlined at `stencil gen`, images base64'd by embed-images.lua -- so this
   button saves the page back to disk with no network fetch. It does not
   build a PDF; see AGENTS.md on why html-to-pdf.js is a separate, container-
   driven step that this control cannot reach into.

   WHY A PARSE-TIME CAPTURE RATHER THAN STRIPPING THE LIVE DOM ON CLICK. By
   the time a reader clicks, hljs has rewritten every code block into spans,
   mermaid has replaced <pre> with rendered SVG, the tab builder has MOVED
   content into panes it created, and a deck has appended a whole toolbar to
   <body>. Those are transformations and insertions a click handler cannot
   cleanly undo by removing nodes. So this partial captures the page's
   pristine head count, the .container element's markup and the two things a
   deck or a Bootstrap component may mutate on <html>/<body> -- all BEFORE
   any of that runs -- and the click handler replaces rather than repairs.

   POSITION IS LOAD-BEARING. Included strictly between _theme-toggle.html.j2
   and _page-scripts.html.j2 in both composition templates:
     - it must run before _page-scripts.html.j2's hljs.highlightAll() and its
       mermaid <pre> -> <div> swap, both of which fire at PARSE time, not on
       DOMContentLoaded -- this is the last moment the body is what pandoc
       wrote (tests/test_download.py's test_download_is_included_between_
       theme_toggle_and_page_scripts pins this);
     - it must run after _theme-toggle.html.j2 so this file's DOMContentLoaded
       listener registers second, which is what puts the download button
       after the theme group in a document's mount host -- listener order,
       not an insertBefore.

   THE PANDOC-LEVEL OBLIGATION THE JINJA CONTRACT TABLE CANNOT SEE.
   tests/test_template_contract.py records the JINJA context keys a shared
   partial reads, and this one reads none -- but its include in both
   composition templates must still sit inside the show_download conditional
   (pandoc's own if/endif around the show_download variable), or the control
   ships on every page regardless of front matter, or a missing closing
   endif silently swallows the includes that follow it. Neither mistake shows
   up in the Jinja contract table, because both are about pandoc's template
   syntax around the include, not about a Jinja variable this file reads.

   THE CSS THIS PARTIAL DEPENDS ON, AND WHERE IT LIVES. .download-button,
   .download-button__label, .theme-toggle-host (its display: flex rule) and
   the @media print entry that hides all three controls are defined in
   _page-style.css.j2, not here. A consuming project that overrides that
   stylesheet while still including this partial gets a visible, unstyled
   downwards-arrow button in the top-right corner of every document -- and,
   because the print rule lives there too, NOT hidden in a printed handout.

   THE GLYPH IS U+2193 (DOWNWARDS ARROW), not a substitute. html-to-pdf.js
   collects the page's rendered text and fails the build on any character its
   inlined fonts cannot draw, and it runs in screen media, where this button
   is visible, so @media print does not shield it there. U+2193 was measured
   against that gate directly and is close to universal in readers' system
   fonts, unlike the rarer arrow glyphs.

   No dollar sign anywhere in this file -- see _theme-toggle.html.j2, which
   this mirrors, and tests/test_download.py's
   test_the_partial_contains_no_pandoc_variable_syntax. That is a CONVENTION,
   not a rule pandoc enforces: a literal dollar in a pandoc template is
   written as a doubled pair, and _page-scripts.html.j2 uses that escape
   deliberately in two regexes. Avoiding it here just means nobody writing
   JavaScript in this file has to notice they are inside a context where a
   single dollar needs doubling -- so no template literals, and no
   end-anchored regex (filename suffix checks below use slice() instead).

   Two mount points, mirroring the theme toggle: a document gets the fixed
   container in html-template.html.j2; a deck's toolbar is built by JS at
   runtime, so _slide-scripts.html.j2 calls mountDownloadButton() once it has
   inserted the theme group, the same way it already calls
   mountThemeToggle(). #}
  <script>
  (function () {
    /* CAPTURED ONCE, AT PARSE TIME, before anything below this partial can
       mutate the page. `download` stays null -- and the button never mounts
       -- if `.container` is missing, which a consumer's own composition
       template is free to spell differently (AGENTS.md documents that
       override). A missing button beats a pageerror, which html-to-pdf.js
       turns into a failed build for every consumer, and beats a button that
       would go on to write a corrupt file. */
    var download = null;
    var PRISTINE = 'data-stencil-pristine';

    try {
      var containerEl = document.querySelector('.container');
      if (containerEl) {
        var liveBodyAttrs = document.body.attributes;
        var bodyAttrs = [];
        for (var i = 0; i < liveBodyAttrs.length; i++) {
          bodyAttrs.push([liveBodyAttrs[i].name, liveBodyAttrs[i].value]);
        }
        /* Captured BEFORE the markers below are stamped, so no marker
           lands inside the string that gets restored on click.

           A STRING, not a detached clone. Chrome live-loads <img> in a
           detached tree, so cloning an image-heavy container would decode
           every base64 image a second time. */
        var containerHtml = containerEl.outerHTML;

        /* MARK what pandoc wrote, rather than counting it or naming its
           classes. Both of the obvious alternatives were tried and both are
           wrong:

           - A child COUNT for <head>, reverted by trimming the tail, assumes
             every later mutation appends. It does not. The vendored mermaid
             bundle prepends its cytoscape stylesheet --
             `a.insertBefore(h, a.children[0])` -- so trimming the tail
             removes the wrong node. Today that node happens to be the
             whitespace before </head> and the damage is invisible; on a
             consumer whose head ends `</style></head>` it is the page's
             entire inlined stylesheet, and the downloaded file opens
             unstyled with no exception and nothing failing.
           - A CLASS whitelist for <body> ties the revert to this
             repository's own markup. AGENTS.md documents composition
             templates as a consumer interface, so a consumer's own wrapper
             would be deleted from every downloaded copy.

           A marker survives cloneNode, does not care in what order anything
           arrives, and is stripped from the clone before it is serialized.
           Scripts are the exception, because the page's own trailing script
           tags are not in the DOM yet at this point -- that is the same fact
           that rules out a child count for <body>. */
        var i;
        var headKids = document.head.children;
        for (i = 0; i < headKids.length; i++) {
          headKids[i].setAttribute(PRISTINE, '');
        }
        var bodyKids = document.body.children;
        for (i = 0; i < bodyKids.length; i++) {
          bodyKids[i].setAttribute(PRISTINE, '');
        }

        /* Every attribute on <html> EXCEPT the two the head bootstrap in
           _page-head.html.j2 has already set by now. Snapshotting those two
           as well would freeze the reader's theme into the download, which
           is the thing being reverted. */
        var rootAttrs = [];
        var liveRootAttrs = document.documentElement.attributes;
        for (i = 0; i < liveRootAttrs.length; i++) {
          var rootName = liveRootAttrs[i].name;
          if (rootName === 'data-theme' || rootName === 'data-theme-pref') continue;
          rootAttrs.push([rootName, liveRootAttrs[i].value]);
        }

        download = {
          containerHtml: containerHtml,
          rootAttrs: rootAttrs,
          bodyAttrs: bodyAttrs,
        };
      }
    } catch (error) {
      download = null;
    }

    /* The filename Chrome's Save dialog would otherwise pick from the
       downloaded blob's own address (none -- it is a blob: URL), derived
       instead from the page's own location so "deck.html" downloads as
       "deck.html" rather than a generated blob id. */
    function byteLength(text) {
      try {
        return new TextEncoder().encode(text).length;
      } catch (error) {
        /* No TextEncoder is not a case any browser this ships to hits, and
           nothing here may throw. Characters are the conservative guess:
           never fewer than the byte count would allow. */
        return text.length;
      }
    }

    /* Trim to a byte budget without splitting a surrogate pair. Iterating
       code points rather than UTF-16 units is what keeps an emoji whole. */
    function truncateToBytes(text, limit) {
      if (byteLength(text) <= limit) return text;
      var chars = typeof Array.from === 'function' ? Array.from(text) : text.split('');
      var out = '';
      for (var i = 0; i < chars.length; i++) {
        if (byteLength(out + chars[i]) > limit) break;
        out += chars[i];
      }
      return out;
    }

    function downloadFilename() {
      var pathname = location.pathname;
      try {
        /* Decode BEFORE splitting on '/'. Splitting first and decoding the
           basename after would let a served %2e%2e%2f path reintroduce a
           path separator post-split. */
        pathname = decodeURIComponent(pathname);
      } catch (error) {
        /* A malformed percent-encoding is left undecoded rather than
           thrown -- nothing here may throw. */
      }

      var slashAt = pathname.lastIndexOf('/');
      var name = slashAt >= 0 ? pathname.slice(slashAt + 1) : pathname;

      /* Strip control characters, the reserved path characters, and the
         bidi control characters that could otherwise reorder how the
         filename renders in a file manager. */
      var reserved = '/\\:*?"<>|';
      var cleaned = '';
      for (var i = 0; i < name.length; i++) {
        var ch = name.charAt(i);
        var code = name.charCodeAt(i);
        var isControl = code <= 0x1F || code === 0x7F;
        var isBidi = (code >= 0x202A && code <= 0x202E) ||
                     (code >= 0x2066 && code <= 0x2069);
        if (isControl || isBidi || reserved.indexOf(ch) !== -1) continue;
        cleaned += ch;
      }

      var start = 0;
      while (start < cleaned.length && cleaned.charAt(start) === '.') start++;
      cleaned = cleaned.slice(start);

      /* A directory-index URL, or a name that was nothing but dots and
         reserved characters, yields an empty string here -- and '' plus
         the appended suffix below would save as "html.html". */
      if (cleaned === '') cleaned = 'document';

      /* The suffix check uses slice(), never indexOf(): indexOf only finds
         ".html" somewhere in the string, so a name that already ends in
         ".html" would still match and pass through unchanged, but a name
         like "html-notes" would falsely look suffixed too. slice() reads
         the actual end of the string, which is the only place a regex
         end-anchor would normally go -- and an end-anchor is the one thing
         off limits in a file with no dollar sign. */
      /* Split any extension the name already carries off the stem rather
         than leaving it attached. Capping a name that ALREADY ends .html
         eats that extension just as surely as capping one this function
         appended -- the suffix has to be out of the way before the stem is
         trimmed, either way. Slicing it off rather than re-spelling it also
         keeps the author's own case, so a .HTML stays .HTML. */
      var lower = cleaned.toLowerCase();
      var suffix = '.html';
      if (lower.slice(-5) === '.html') {
        suffix = cleaned.slice(-5);
        cleaned = cleaned.slice(0, -5);
      } else if (lower.slice(-4) === '.htm') {
        suffix = cleaned.slice(-4);
        cleaned = cleaned.slice(0, -4);
      }

      /* Measured against real Chrome: a name over roughly 255 BYTES makes
         the download get silently canceled -- the click appears to do
         nothing and nothing anywhere reports an error. 200 bytes leaves
         headroom under that.

         The cap goes on the STEM, before the suffix is appended. Capping
         the finished name instead truncates the extension it just added: a
         210-character path yields a 200-character name ending in no
         extension at all, which does not open in a browser on a double
         click and gives the reader nothing to go on. Measured, deterministic
         and ASCII-only -- it needs nothing more exotic than a long filename.

         Bytes rather than characters, and trimmed on a code point boundary,
         because 90 CJK characters is 275 UTF-8 bytes -- already past the
         threshold this cap exists to stay under -- and a character-wise
         slice can leave a lone surrogate at the end. */
      return truncateToBytes(cleaned, 200 - byteLength(suffix)) + suffix;
    }

    /* The click handler. Nothing in here may throw -- html-to-pdf.js treats
       an uncaught pageerror as a failed build, and show_download now
       defaults to on, so every consumer's build depends on this. */
    function downloadSelf() {
      if (!download) return;
      try {
        /* Clone document.documentElement FIRST, before creating the
           transient anchor below -- cloning after would serialize that
           anchor into the copy. */
        var clone = document.documentElement.cloneNode(true);

        /* Restore <html>'s attributes wholesale from the snapshot. That
           drops data-theme and data-theme-pref (deliberately excluded when
           the snapshot was taken), drops the `presenting` class a deck adds,
           and also drops whatever some future library decides to put here --
           which naming the two theme attributes explicitly would not. */
        var cloneRootAttrs = Array.prototype.slice.call(clone.attributes);
        cloneRootAttrs.forEach(function (attr) {
          clone.removeAttribute(attr.name);
        });
        download.rootAttrs.forEach(function (pair) {
          clone.setAttribute(pair[0], pair[1]);
        });

        /* Keep the head elements pandoc wrote, drop the rest. Text nodes are
           left alone: only an element can carry the marker, and only
           elements get injected. */
        var cloneHead = clone.querySelector('head');
        if (cloneHead) {
          Array.prototype.slice.call(cloneHead.children).forEach(function (child) {
            if (!child.hasAttribute(PRISTINE)) cloneHead.removeChild(child);
          });
        }

        var cloneBody = clone.querySelector('body');
        if (cloneBody) {
          /* A WHITELIST, not a denylist, and measured rather than
             defensive: mermaid appends a div.mermaidTooltip directly to
             <body> on every page that draws a diagram, outside .container
             and outside <head>. A deck's toolbar lands here too, as a
             consequence of the same rule rather than a special case, and so
             will whatever a future release appends. Truncating the body by
             a child count -- the trick used for <head> above -- would be
             wrong here: this partial runs mid-parse, so _page-scripts.html.j2
             and _slide-scripts.html.j2 are not yet in the DOM, and the count
             would be short -- truncating would strip the page's own scripts
             out of the downloaded copy. */
          var children = Array.prototype.slice.call(cloneBody.children);
          children.forEach(function (child) {
            /* Marked, or a script. The page's own trailing script tags were
               not in the DOM when the markers were stamped -- this partial
               runs mid-parse -- which is exactly why <body> cannot be
               reverted by a child count the way <head> once was. */
            var keep = child.hasAttribute(PRISTINE) || child.tagName === 'SCRIPT';
            if (!keep) {
              cloneBody.removeChild(child);
            } else if (child.classList.contains('theme-toggle-host')) {
              /* Empty even when it survives: show_download's own control
                 mounts into this same host. */
              while (child.firstChild) child.removeChild(child.firstChild);
            }
          });

          /* Replace the container AFTER the marker sweep above, never
             before it. The captured markup was taken before the markers
             were stamped, so it carries none -- splice it in first and the
             sweep sees an unmarked body child and deletes the entire
             document. That is not hypothetical: it is what the first
             version of this did, and the downloaded deck came out with no
             slides at all.

             The setter works on this detached clone because the element
             still has a parent -- the cloned body -- so it does not throw,
             and it does not execute any script in the replacement markup. */
          var cloneContainer = cloneBody.querySelector('.container');
          if (cloneContainer) {
            cloneContainer.outerHTML = download.containerHtml;
          }

          /* Restore exactly what pandoc wrote, dropping whatever runtime
             code added -- a Bootstrap component that sets class="modal-open"
             and an inline padding-right on <body> is the case nothing else
             here would catch. */
          var currentBodyAttrs = Array.prototype.slice.call(cloneBody.attributes);
          currentBodyAttrs.forEach(function (attr) {
            cloneBody.removeAttribute(attr.name);
          });
          download.bodyAttrs.forEach(function (pair) {
            cloneBody.setAttribute(pair[0], pair[1]);
          });
        }

        /* The markers are this partial's bookkeeping, not the page's.
           Strip them from the clone so the downloaded file is what pandoc
           wrote, and so downloading again from that copy behaves the same. */
        Array.prototype.slice.call(clone.querySelectorAll('[' + PRISTINE + ']'))
          .forEach(function (marked) {
            marked.removeAttribute(PRISTINE);
          });

        var html = '<!DOCTYPE html>\n' + clone.outerHTML;
        var filename = downloadFilename();
        var blob = new Blob([html], { type: 'text/html;charset=utf-8' });
        var url = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
        var link = document.createElement('a');
        try {
          link.href = url;
          link.download = filename;
          document.body.appendChild(link);
          link.click();
        } finally {
          /* Both cleanups in the finally, not after the click. A throw
             between here and there would otherwise pin a multi-megabyte blob
             for the life of the tab, and leave a stray anchor in the live
             DOM for the next capture to trip over. */
          if (link.parentNode) link.parentNode.removeChild(link);
          URL.revokeObjectURL(url);
        }
      } catch (error) {
        /* Nothing may throw out of a click handler on a page html-to-pdf.js
           might load. */
      }
    }

    window.mountDownloadButton = function (container) {
      if (!container || !download || container.querySelector('.download-button')) return;

      var button = document.createElement('button');
      button.type = 'button';
      button.className = 'download-button';
      button.title = 'Download';
      /* The glyph is decorative -- the same pattern _theme-toggle.html.j2
         uses for its three segments -- so the accessible name comes from
         the visually-hidden label rather than a character nobody can
         pronounce. */
      button.innerHTML =
        '<span aria-hidden="true">↓</span>' +
        '<span class="download-button__label">Download</span>';
      button.addEventListener('click', downloadSelf);

      container.appendChild(button);
      return button;
    };

    /* Documents mount immediately, into the same host the theme toggle
       uses. Registered AFTER _theme-toggle.html.j2's own listener, which is
       what puts this button after the theme group -- listener order, not an
       insertBefore. A deck has no such host; _slide-scripts.html.j2 calls
       mountDownloadButton() itself once its toolbar exists. */
    document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function () {
      var host = document.querySelector('.theme-toggle-host');
      if (host) window.mountDownloadButton(host);
    });
  })();
  </script>
